WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.267 --> 00:00:02.435
-Our next guest
is a comedian and actor

00:00:02.435 --> 00:00:03.669
who is Emmy-nominated
for her work

00:00:03.669 --> 00:00:05.572
in the critically acclaimed
series "Hacks,"

00:00:05.572 --> 00:00:08.141
which is streaming on HBO Max.
Let's take look.

00:00:08.141 --> 00:00:10.310
-Sell it to me.
-Are you out of your mind?

00:00:10.310 --> 00:00:12.111
It's one of a kind.
-Sell me it!

00:00:12.111 --> 00:00:13.680
I have nothing left to lose!
I'll do it!

00:00:13.680 --> 00:00:15.582
-Stop. Put it down.
-Sell me it!

00:00:15.582 --> 00:00:17.250
-You think you have nothing
in common with Deborah Vance?

00:00:17.250 --> 00:00:18.751
I know you're the same.

00:00:18.751 --> 00:00:20.253
You're both psychotic bitches!

00:00:20.253 --> 00:00:21.887
-You have three seconds
to take the money,

00:00:21.887 --> 00:00:23.055
or I'm gonna do it!
-Please think of the

00:00:23.055 --> 00:00:24.390
Obenchain-McMillan family!
-What?!

00:00:24.390 --> 00:00:26.425
I don't give a [bleep]
about the Obenchain-McMillans!

00:00:26.425 --> 00:00:30.330
They probably owned slaves!
[Bleep] you, Lemony Snicket!

00:00:30.330 --> 00:00:32.966
-Please welcome to the show,
Hannah Einbinder.

00:00:32.966 --> 00:00:35.101
[ Applause ]

00:00:35.101 --> 00:00:36.469 line:0%
♪♪

00:00:36.469 --> 00:00:37.703
-Thank you so much
for being here!

00:00:37.703 --> 00:00:39.739
-Thank you for having me.
-Welcome.

00:00:39.739 --> 00:00:41.741
[ Applause ]
-[ Chuckles ]

00:00:41.741 --> 00:00:44.844 line:0%
♪♪

00:00:44.844 --> 00:00:46.578
-Hi.
-Hi! I'm so happy you're here.

00:00:46.578 --> 00:00:47.647
-Oh, my God, likewise.

00:00:47.647 --> 00:00:48.982
-Congratulations on your
wonderful show.

00:00:48.982 --> 00:00:51.517
And I feel a little bad,
because the show is --

00:00:51.517 --> 00:00:52.985
You know,
it's about stand-up comedy.

00:00:52.985 --> 00:00:56.356
It's about how exciting it is
to be in front of an audience.

00:00:56.356 --> 00:00:57.523
And I do wish
you could've been here

00:00:57.523 --> 00:00:58.924
on a night
where we had more people.

00:00:58.924 --> 00:01:01.961
-Oh, no. I -- 10 -- 10 guys
in the dark is home for me.

00:01:01.961 --> 00:01:03.763
[ Laughter ]

00:01:03.763 --> 00:01:06.332
I --
[ Laughter ]

00:01:06.332 --> 00:01:08.201
Target demo.
-This is your --

00:01:08.201 --> 00:01:10.236
Because now you --
Because you've been a stand-up

00:01:10.236 --> 00:01:12.171
for a very long time.
-Yes.

00:01:12.171 --> 00:01:13.739
-This is a wonderful show
about stand-up,

00:01:13.739 --> 00:01:15.141
about what it means
to be a stand-up.

00:01:15.141 --> 00:01:17.343
But as someone who's been
a stand-up for a very long time,

00:01:17.343 --> 00:01:20.113
you have played in rooms where
there weren't a lot of people.

00:01:20.113 --> 00:01:24.917
-Yes, yes.
I -- I actually have played for,

00:01:24.917 --> 00:01:27.253
if you can believe it,
negative amounts of people.

00:01:27.253 --> 00:01:28.688
-What -- No,
what is negative amounts?

00:01:28.688 --> 00:01:30.156
-Well, like, in a building
like this --

00:01:30.156 --> 00:01:33.760
Like, this is 30 Rock, so, like,
I have done shows that maybe,

00:01:33.760 --> 00:01:36.462
like, on nights where the only
people in the audience would be,

00:01:36.462 --> 00:01:39.231
like, ghosts with wacky wigs.
[ Laughter ]

00:01:39.231 --> 00:01:40.867
-Right, sure.
The ghost audience.

00:01:40.867 --> 00:01:44.236
-Yeah, just when no one shows up
and you kind of just

00:01:44.236 --> 00:01:46.405
barrel through, right down.
-So, you're obviously being

00:01:46.405 --> 00:01:49.575
critically acclaimed
for this role, Emmy-nominated.

00:01:49.575 --> 00:01:52.879
Has that shown up yet,
insofar as more people

00:01:52.879 --> 00:01:54.780
coming to see you
when you're doing sets?

00:01:54.780 --> 00:01:55.915
'Cause you're still doing
stand-up.

00:01:55.915 --> 00:01:58.951
-Yeah, I mean, yes and no.
Like -- if --

00:01:58.951 --> 00:02:01.121
You know, I do, like,
alt-shows in L.A. sometimes,

00:02:01.121 --> 00:02:03.823
so, like -- I mean, like,
you know, when --

00:02:03.823 --> 00:02:06.459
when we got the news
that we were nominated

00:02:06.459 --> 00:02:10.029
in this lovely way,
that was at 8:00 a.m.,

00:02:10.029 --> 00:02:12.732
and then at 8:00 p.m.,
I, you know,

00:02:12.732 --> 00:02:15.034
bombed for seven people,

00:02:15.034 --> 00:02:17.036
several feet away
from a dumpster, so...

00:02:17.036 --> 00:02:18.704
[ Laughter ]

00:02:18.704 --> 00:02:20.906
-Keeps you humble.
-♪ City of stars ♪

00:02:20.906 --> 00:02:23.910
Yeah, it really -- It really --
So, it keeps you --

00:02:23.910 --> 00:02:25.478
It keeps you --
'Cause stand-up doesn't really,

00:02:25.478 --> 00:02:27.247
I feel like it's so honest.

00:02:27.247 --> 00:02:30.483
It doesn't care who you are.
-Right.

00:02:30.483 --> 00:02:35.288
-And doing it in L.A., you know,
their audiences are kind of

00:02:35.288 --> 00:02:39.758
just like, "Oh, you're an actor?

00:02:39.758 --> 00:02:41.627
So are all of us."
[ Laughter ]

00:02:41.627 --> 00:02:43.129
"Oh, you got an Emmy nom?
Yeah.

00:02:43.129 --> 00:02:44.563
Chelsea Peretti
was here last week.

00:02:44.563 --> 00:02:46.732
She [bleep] killed,
so good luck."

00:02:46.732 --> 00:02:50.670
-Well, I guess that is the --
sort of a trial by fire.

00:02:50.670 --> 00:02:52.705
Like, that is probably
very helpful.

00:02:52.705 --> 00:02:54.741
And you end up getting to work
with Jean Smart.

00:02:54.741 --> 00:02:57.543
Jean was here
singing your praises.

00:02:57.543 --> 00:03:00.413
But also, strikes me as like --
in a way that I think

00:03:00.413 --> 00:03:03.182
anybody who would work with her
would want her to be.

00:03:03.182 --> 00:03:05.618
She also seems to, like,
bust chops a fair amount.

00:03:05.618 --> 00:03:09.355
-Oh, yeah. Yeah.
I invited her to one of my --

00:03:09.355 --> 00:03:12.025
one of my shows in L.A., and I
was telling her about the show,

00:03:12.025 --> 00:03:13.726
and I was saying, you know,

00:03:13.726 --> 00:03:16.295
"Natalie Palamides
and Courtney Pauroso,"

00:03:16.295 --> 00:03:19.432
who are clowns, which,
for those who don't know,

00:03:19.432 --> 00:03:21.367
clowning is a form
of performance art.

00:03:21.367 --> 00:03:26.839
It's a comedic, uh, uh,
performance, um, art.

00:03:26.839 --> 00:03:28.641
Good. We got it all.
[ Laughter ]

00:03:28.641 --> 00:03:30.276
Just want to get it out.

00:03:30.276 --> 00:03:31.511
-You're not gonna get heat
from the clown community.

00:03:31.511 --> 00:03:33.011
I think you laid it out.
[ Laughter ]

00:03:33.011 --> 00:03:34.180
-Sorry.
-With a lot of integrity.

00:03:34.180 --> 00:03:36.215
-Sorry. They're very nice.
But they --

00:03:36.215 --> 00:03:38.851
I said, "You know, they're
opening for me tonight.

00:03:38.851 --> 00:03:40.119
I have two clowns
opening for me."

00:03:40.119 --> 00:03:42.121
And she said, "Oh, great.
So three on the bill."

00:03:42.121 --> 00:03:44.924
[ Laughter ]

00:03:44.924 --> 00:03:46.959
-Jean and I have
this awesome little joke

00:03:46.959 --> 00:03:49.762
where she's mean to me,
and I'm in love with her.

00:03:49.762 --> 00:03:52.364
It's --
[ Laughter ]

00:03:52.364 --> 00:03:54.800
-Do you -- I mean, has it been,

00:03:54.800 --> 00:03:56.903
since you met all these people
and you're so close

00:03:56.903 --> 00:03:58.370
to these people now,
have they --

00:03:58.370 --> 00:04:00.573
I mean, did she actually
come when you told her clowns

00:04:00.573 --> 00:04:02.809
were opening for you?
Or did she --

00:04:02.809 --> 00:04:04.376
-Yeah.
-Okay, that's great.

00:04:04.376 --> 00:04:06.646
-I mean, she -- she keeps --
keeps a low profile in the back,

00:04:06.646 --> 00:04:08.714
but she's --
she's so supportive,

00:04:08.714 --> 00:04:12.552
and all the cast, really, like,
we all talk all the time.

00:04:12.552 --> 00:04:14.387
We have a little group chat.
-That's great.

00:04:14.387 --> 00:04:16.121
-And we're checking in.
It's really lovely.

00:04:16.121 --> 00:04:17.589
-Has anything --
This was also --

00:04:17.589 --> 00:04:20.326
it should be noted, which is,
you know, hard to believe

00:04:20.326 --> 00:04:22.228
because you're so good and so
natural in this, but this is

00:04:22.228 --> 00:04:25.298
kind of your first acting job.
And, do you --

00:04:25.298 --> 00:04:27.266
I mean, has it changed
a lot of stuff

00:04:27.266 --> 00:04:30.603
other than the fact that you
have your Emmy nomination?

00:04:30.603 --> 00:04:32.805
-Um, yeah.
I mean, it's --

00:04:32.805 --> 00:04:36.675
I found, like, this really
beautiful community online,

00:04:36.675 --> 00:04:39.679
which has been really lovely,
because, like, I have

00:04:39.679 --> 00:04:42.048
pretty low self-esteem,

00:04:42.048 --> 00:04:44.150
and I can be pretty critical
of myself.

00:04:44.150 --> 00:04:46.819
So when people online are like,
"I hate Hannah,"

00:04:46.819 --> 00:04:50.089
I'm like, "Okay. I feel that."
[ Laughter ]

00:04:50.089 --> 00:04:53.125
-"Yes, my community, my people."
-That's so nice.

00:04:53.125 --> 00:04:54.460
-It's awesome to be...

00:04:54.460 --> 00:04:56.495
-Did you -- Did you pull
from people you met?

00:04:56.495 --> 00:04:57.896
Because, again,
you mention you're in L.A.,

00:04:57.896 --> 00:05:00.933
so you know a lot of comedy
writers, a lot of comedians.

00:05:00.933 --> 00:05:02.401
When you were putting
the character together,

00:05:02.401 --> 00:05:04.904
were you pulling from yourself
or others, as well?

00:05:04.904 --> 00:05:06.238
-I've got to say, you know,

00:05:06.238 --> 00:05:08.140
the writers
did such a beautiful job.

00:05:08.140 --> 00:05:09.742
She was so vivid.

00:05:09.742 --> 00:05:12.611
She was really all there
when I first encountered her.

00:05:12.611 --> 00:05:15.881
But I think what I uniquely
bring to Ava is

00:05:15.881 --> 00:05:19.218
probably cystic acne, red hair,
and no upper-body strength.

00:05:19.218 --> 00:05:20.586
-Oh, that's great.
[ Laughter ]

00:05:20.586 --> 00:05:21.854
-That was important.
-So, you ticked --

00:05:21.854 --> 00:05:23.756
you ticked all the boxes
they were looking for there.

00:05:23.756 --> 00:05:24.957
-Yeah.
And then, they're going,

00:05:24.957 --> 00:05:26.258
"Oh, wow. I didn't
really see this at first,

00:05:26.258 --> 00:05:27.493
and now it couldn't
be any other way."

00:05:27.493 --> 00:05:28.961
[ Laughter ]

00:05:28.961 --> 00:05:33.665
-You -- You've been a fan of
award shows your whole life.

00:05:33.665 --> 00:05:37.269
You've had some thematic
award-based parties.

00:05:37.269 --> 00:05:40.239
-I have.
God, no other way to say this

00:05:40.239 --> 00:05:43.976
than just directly,
but my bat mitzvah theme was,

00:05:43.976 --> 00:05:46.646
"Hannah Einbinder's
First Annual Friendship Awards."

00:05:46.646 --> 00:05:48.414
[ Laughter ]

00:05:48.414 --> 00:05:51.483
And I --

00:05:51.483 --> 00:05:54.086
I did -- I did -- I did give out
awards to my friends.

00:05:54.086 --> 00:05:56.189
-Gotcha.
-And...

00:05:56.189 --> 00:05:58.357
-Were they --
Were they uniquely --

00:05:58.357 --> 00:06:00.026
Were they uniquely tailored
to each friend,

00:06:00.026 --> 00:06:01.527
what kind of friendship award
they got?

00:06:01.527 --> 00:06:02.762
-Yes.
-Okay.

00:06:02.762 --> 00:06:04.163
-It was --
Because I'm from L.A.,

00:06:04.163 --> 00:06:06.431
it was like, "Everybody's
got to feel, like, special.

00:06:06.431 --> 00:06:09.201
It can't be -- you know."
-Right.

00:06:09.201 --> 00:06:11.070
-"No one can feel
like they were snubbed."

00:06:11.070 --> 00:06:13.005
[ Laughter ]

00:06:13.005 --> 00:06:18.010
-Did you have any other before
you got a job as an actor?

00:06:18.010 --> 00:06:19.979
I'm assuming you had other
lesser jobs

00:06:19.979 --> 00:06:22.014
getting through
your early stand-up years.

00:06:22.014 --> 00:06:24.784
-Yeah.
I was a barista...

00:06:24.784 --> 00:06:26.752
Okay.
-...in the epicenter

00:06:26.752 --> 00:06:30.222
of douchebaggery in Los Angeles.

00:06:30.222 --> 00:06:33.659
And -- I -- I had
lovely co-workers

00:06:33.659 --> 00:06:36.294
who are still my -- my best
friends to this day.

00:06:36.294 --> 00:06:38.130
But, you know, you --

00:06:38.130 --> 00:06:40.066
It was a job that worked for me
at the time.

00:06:40.066 --> 00:06:43.102
I was able to, you know,
get absolutely ripped

00:06:43.102 --> 00:06:45.938
off caffeine during the day,
and then, you know,

00:06:45.938 --> 00:06:49.074
perform at open mics
in an almond-milk-soaked flannel

00:06:49.074 --> 00:06:50.109
by night.
-[ Laughs ]

00:06:50.109 --> 00:06:52.078
-And so that worked
with my schedule.

00:06:52.078 --> 00:06:54.813
But, yeah, we --
It's --

00:06:54.813 --> 00:06:56.849
Look, when you are in this body

00:06:56.849 --> 00:06:59.285
and working
in the service industry,

00:06:59.285 --> 00:07:01.086
you encounter men who kind of

00:07:01.086 --> 00:07:03.689
take advantage
of the power structure.

00:07:03.689 --> 00:07:06.859
-Sure.
-I had this one customer who --

00:07:06.859 --> 00:07:09.128
He came in once and, you know,
he ordered a tea.

00:07:09.128 --> 00:07:11.663
And I said, do you -- you know,
would you like it sweetened?

00:07:11.663 --> 00:07:13.132
And he said, "Oh, yeah,
I'd love it sweetened.

00:07:13.132 --> 00:07:16.268
Just stick your pinky in it."

00:07:16.268 --> 00:07:20.072
Yeah, so I brewed his tea,
and then I just --

00:07:20.072 --> 00:07:21.240
I looked right at him

00:07:21.240 --> 00:07:23.042
and I just sort of fisted
his earl grey.

00:07:23.042 --> 00:07:24.577
[ Laughter ]

00:07:24.577 --> 00:07:28.414
-That's the right -- I think
that's very, very appropriate.

00:07:28.414 --> 00:07:29.848
Hey, congratulations
on the show,

00:07:29.848 --> 00:07:30.850
and thanks so much
for being here.

00:07:30.850 --> 00:07:32.250
-Thank you.
-Lovely to meet you.

00:07:32.250 --> 00:07:33.385
-Oh, my God, likewise.

00:07:33.385 --> 00:07:34.887
-"Hacks" is streaming
on HBO Max.

